SE Radio is brought to you by the IEEE Computer Society and by IEEE Software Magazine online at computer.org/software.

3
00:00:21,390 --> 00:00:24,030
For Software Engineering Radio, this is Robert Blumen.

4
00:00:24,030 --> 00:00:26,710
I have with me Chris Love.

5
00:00:26,710 --> 00:00:28,790
Chris works at Modernize as a distinguished engineer.

6
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:37,390
Chris and Jay Vyas are co-authors of the book Core Kubernetes and Chris is a Google Cloud Certified Fellow.

7
00:00:37,390 --> 00:00:40,230
Chris, welcome to Software Engineering Radio.

8
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:42,510
Thank you, Robert.

9
00:00:42,510 --> 00:00:46,391
Really appreciate you having me come in and speak with you folks today. Happy to have you.

10
00:00:46,391 --> 00:00:52,230
We're going to take advantage of your being here to talk about Kubernetes security.

11
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:58,750
Now, Kubernetes is a distributed system except external traffic.

12
00:00:58,750 --> 00:01:01,190
It uses compute resources.

13
00:01:01,190 --> 00:01:09,070
Is there such a thing as Kubernetes security or is it just about following best practices that are well-known for these type of systems?

14
00:01:09,070 --> 00:01:11,710
I think it's both.

15
00:01:11,710 --> 00:01:18,670
I think, of course, it's best practices like update your software dependencies, update your dependencies from your operating system.

16
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:29,270
It's that, but now you're running a containerized system, so you have to remember to update the dependencies in the container as well as update your host version of Bash.

17
00:01:29,270 --> 00:01:31,710
So, of course, there's intricacies to it.

18
00:01:31,710 --> 00:01:43,510
We're running a nice distributed system that allows us to do complicated stuff like scaling and we have failover, but because of that we've got a little bit more complicated networking that can cause some challenges from a security standpoint.

19
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:47,590
But, we have other distributed systems that we've been using for a while.

20
00:01:47,590 --> 00:01:51,550
Most of them are based around containers, but there's definitely some wrinkles.

21
00:01:51,550 --> 00:01:55,550
But, like you said, at the end of the day, it's an API layer.

22
00:01:55,550 --> 00:02:07,510
You've got a bunch of compute nodes, which are either your servers or your EC2 instances or your GKE instances and you've got a bunch of containers running around.

23
00:02:07,510 --> 00:02:12,350
So, I would say it's non-trivial, but it's not rocket science.

24
00:02:12,350 --> 00:02:17,390
It's not as challenging as catching a rocket with a couple chopsticks.

25
00:02:17,390 --> 00:02:24,310
The overall umbrella of Kubernetes security, it has many subtopics, more than we can cover in an hour.

26
00:02:24,310 --> 00:02:29,750
If you were to pick a few that are most important to focus on, what's your short list?

27
00:02:29,750 --> 00:02:36,710
I would try to group them into I kind of go from large world internal.

28
00:02:36,710 --> 00:02:42,070
So, we could talk about what to expect when setting up a Kubernetes cluster, so overall security.

29
00:02:42,070 --> 00:02:51,070
Then, you can go down to a node level security from there, network security from there, pod security and from there, container security.

30
00:02:51,070 --> 00:02:57,790
Container security is well-documented, but I think some folks either don't have the time and money to put those in place.

31
00:02:57,790 --> 00:03:01,270
Operating system security, I'm not going to talk to you about.

32
00:03:01,270 --> 00:03:06,790
There's lots of other references that folks can go to that I always look at myself as a Lego engineer, right?

33
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:14,550
We've got building blocks. Some are uniques to Kubernetes and like you said, some operating system security is operating system security.

34
00:03:14,550 --> 00:03:21,790
But, typically on a operating system, you aren't running two different network layers and that's what you get within Kubernetes.

35
00:03:21,790 --> 00:03:29,390
So, it'd be a good time for me to let the listeners know we did an entire episode on Kubernetes networking, number 619.

36
00:03:29,390 --> 00:03:31,590
We will return to that a little bit later.

37
00:03:31,590 --> 00:03:38,270
Let's go down your list, Chris, and hit these things in order starting with setting up Kubernetes overall security.

38
00:03:38,270 --> 00:03:42,430
What are some of the main points that should be addressed?

39
00:03:42,430 --> 00:03:50,030
Right, you want to think about from a network layer, from a node setup layer and from a overall account permissions layer.

40
00:03:50,030 --> 00:03:52,870
Again, if you're running in a data center, this is a little bit different, right?

41
00:03:52,870 --> 00:04:03,510
But, I would say majority of people that are running a Kubernetes are running within AWS or GKE or Azure or pick your cloud provider.

42
00:04:03,510 --> 00:04:05,870
So, there's always some gotchas around those cloud environments.

43
00:04:05,880 --> 00:04:16,229
For instance, you want to make sure that the role that you're setting up your cluster with and the role that the cluster's running with are the correct roles.

44
00:04:16,229 --> 00:04:23,270
You don't want to set your cluster up with a role that is a account level admin.

45
00:04:23,270 --> 00:04:26,910
You want to give your Kubernetes cluster and your Kubernetes nodes the right level of permissions.

46
00:04:26,920 --> 00:04:31,830
So, that means setting up a user before you set up Kubernetes.

47
00:04:31,830 --> 00:04:34,590
From there, also look at a private network.

48
00:04:34,590 --> 00:04:36,990
Don't expose your nodes to the public.

49
00:04:36,990 --> 00:04:43,150
In other words, port 22 on node A should not be accessible via an external network.

50
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:53,150
You're going to need to VPN into your nodes and realistically, developers and admins shouldn't necessarily have to add a node level.

51
00:04:53,150 --> 00:04:56,950
Also, your API layer or web API should also be behind a firewall.

52
00:04:56,950 --> 00:05:09,870
That should be networked in and should be on a private network where folks aren't able to access and people we've had bugs in Kubernetes where authentication for very short amount of time was broken at the API layer.

53
00:05:09,870 --> 00:05:14,190
And fortunately, folks that maintain Kubernetes fixed it pretty fast,

54
00:05:14,190 --> 00:05:23,830
but it was overnight where if you had a publicly exposed API and you had XYZ version of Kubernetes, people could just do a coup control command right to it.

55
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:33,190
So, doing some basic setup before you are thinking through your security model and your security setup before you set up a cluster is really important.

56
00:05:33,190 --> 00:05:37,550
IP spacing, for instance, that gets folks in trouble as well.

57
00:05:37,550 --> 00:05:42,070
So, you want to make sure the subnet you're running on is private.

58
00:05:42,070 --> 00:05:48,270
Main points there would be use the cloud service provider's access management,

59
00:05:48,270 --> 00:05:55,871
use a private VPC and put your whole cluster behind some kind of firewall or proxy to cut it off from the web. Did I miss anything?

60
00:05:55,871 --> 00:05:59,550
No, and then you're looking at some form of public ingress, right?

61
00:05:59,550 --> 00:06:12,150
You're looking at having to maintain some form of ingress controller that allow external traffic to go in internally into your cluster, which makes it a little bit more complicated, but I would rather not have nodes exposed to the internet personally.

62
00:06:12,160 --> 00:06:19,390
Would any of the last set of answers change if you are running the cluster on the company's own computers?

63
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:22,870
You can think about it in the same way, right?

64
00:06:22,870 --> 00:06:30,191
You would have some sort of router setup where you again have internal IP addresses and external IP addresses. It's just more work.

65
00:06:30,191 --> 00:06:44,230
You have to set up your air quote VPC network or network that's internal by hand as and maintain it and you can't just make a Terraform call to your cloud provider's API to set it up for you.

66
00:06:44,230 --> 00:06:48,350
You have to have yourself or somebody else set those routers up.

67
00:06:48,350 --> 00:06:50,390
But, same type of model exists.

68
00:06:50,390 --> 00:06:53,030
Networking is networking is networking.

69
00:06:53,030 --> 00:06:55,070
We have eight IP addresses here and there.

70
00:06:55,070 --> 00:07:04,110
Do you have any stories from a time you were setting up a cluster, something you missed or from auditing another setup where you found something that needed to be locked down?

71
00:07:04,120 --> 00:07:06,910
Yeah, many of those.

72
00:07:06,910 --> 00:07:10,870
I've found pretty much everything that I've talked about set up incorrectly.

73
00:07:10,870 --> 00:07:19,910
A lot of the times, people set up their cluster and exposed API server and or the nodes or both publicly.

74
00:07:19,910 --> 00:07:24,710
It's more complicated to set up a VPN type mechanism to get into your nodes.

75
00:07:24,710 --> 00:07:27,870
It's much easier not to have it that way, right?

76
00:07:27,870 --> 00:07:42,471
As well as I've seen many clusters that were created using like I have a cloud access a cloud admin account and I incorrectly set up my cluster and it is using Chris Love's cloud admin account. That's not good.

77
00:07:42,480 --> 00:07:54,790
Being able to have a Kubernetes cluster create a VPC or create other components isn't a great way to do security within Kubernetes.

78
00:07:54,790 --> 00:07:59,710
I am both authorization and both auth and off Z are complicated enough.

79
00:07:59,710 --> 00:08:04,510
You don't want to add a layer where you're running your cluster as a Chris Love user.

80
00:08:04,520 --> 00:08:07,070
I've set up a cluster.

81
00:08:07,070 --> 00:08:12,310
I went through my checklist, done all the things we talked about.

82
00:08:12,310 --> 00:08:21,870
Are there any either open source frameworks or testing tools that would run through and verify that the things that need access have and the things that should not have access cannot?

83
00:08:21,870 --> 00:08:28,830
I believe the CNCF has a testing tool that goes through your cluster soup to nuts, kind of gives it once over.

84
00:08:28,830 --> 00:08:35,431
Also, frankly, the documentation on Kubernetes security on the Kubernetes sites tremendous. So, I'm going through.

85
00:08:35,431 --> 00:08:46,430
It almost drills down in the same order that I've been using in a long time and I can't say I've written any of the security documentation there, but it has a checklist of what to do.

86
00:08:46,430 --> 00:08:52,870
You've got tools that are owned or been donated and maintained by other companies that CNCF have.

87
00:08:52,870 --> 00:08:58,830
And there's plenty of companies there outside that can either help you set them up or test them after the fact.

88
00:08:58,840 --> 00:09:01,790
Sounds great.

89
00:09:01,790 --> 00:09:07,070
I think we can move on to the next layer that you addressed, which is node security.

90
00:09:07,070 --> 00:09:09,750
What are some of the common attacks at the node level?

91
00:09:09,760 --> 00:09:16,591
Again, it's instance or server level security. It's SSH attacks, right?

92
00:09:16,600 --> 00:09:26,550
Fortunately, our modern-day cloud providers give us good authentication methods for SSH that is not username and password.

93
00:09:26,550 --> 00:09:28,550
But, again, it goes back to you're maintaining nodes.

94
00:09:28,550 --> 00:09:34,070
I would say this, unless you're running a data center, you're not looking at in-place upgrades.

95
00:09:34,070 --> 00:09:37,790
And if you're running in your own data center, you could be running virtualization as well.

96
00:09:37,790 --> 00:09:46,150
And if you're virtualized and you have your own cloud, we're looking at doing rollovers of an operating system to upgrade your OS.

97
00:09:46,160 --> 00:09:52,630
We're also looking at running immutable operating systems, which is another thing that I highly recommend.

98
00:09:52,630 --> 00:10:02,350
Running operating systems that have read-only components, it allows you to not have bad guys overwrite binaries.

99
00:10:02,350 --> 00:10:04,550
You know, that's just a bad thing.

100
00:10:04,550 --> 00:10:13,470
But again, in terms of how Kubernetes upgrades, rather than upgrading your OS, you need to upgrade a node.

101
00:10:13,470 --> 00:10:18,070
So, upgrade your nodes through a rolling upgrade, and then you update your operating system.

102
00:10:18,070 --> 00:10:24,590
If you're running on bare metal without virtualization, then most likely you are looking at in-place upgrades.

103
00:10:24,590 --> 00:10:28,750
But then you can shift around your workloads to do that.

104
00:10:28,750 --> 00:10:31,790
As we mentioned at the top of the show, OS level security is OS.

105
00:10:31,790 --> 00:10:39,950
It's really the I would say that's probably the best-known security posture that we know of, right?

106
00:10:39,950 --> 00:10:48,670
Because system admins have been maintaining OS level security since the beginning of time, at least the beginning of Unix, and the beginning of computers.

107
00:10:48,670 --> 00:10:55,350
So, that's probably the best-known security layer or the best-known security practices that we have.

108
00:10:55,350 --> 00:11:01,270
Now, as we move into the network security, we move into the pod security, and we move into container security, I would say those are all the newer technologies.

109
00:11:01,280 --> 00:11:11,950
If I'm running on a cloud service provider, then they manage the nodes, they will auto-scale nodes in or out of the cluster as needed.

110
00:11:11,950 --> 00:11:18,590
Can I count on the cloud service provider to manage the node image and to refresh it if vulnerabilities are discovered,

111
00:11:18,590 --> 00:11:27,270
or is that something that as a cluster operator I need to push a button or do something when I want a refresh of the node image?

112
00:11:27,270 --> 00:11:29,470
It really depends on how you set up your cluster.

113
00:11:29,470 --> 00:11:33,790
First off, you can run your own control plane for Kubernetes.

114
00:11:33,790 --> 00:11:36,070
Lots of companies that are a little bit more sophisticated do that.

115
00:11:36,080 --> 00:11:44,190
They'll still run their own master instances with etcd, etc., etc. So, sometimes the control plane is maintained, sometimes it's not.

116
00:11:44,190 --> 00:11:52,950
I would say the majority of companies that are running on top of EKS and GCP or AKS, they are using a managed control plane.

117
00:11:52,960 --> 00:12:03,510
Nodes, on the other hand, you often have options that the cloud provider automatically upgrades for you, or you have to upgrade them yourselves.

118
00:12:03,510 --> 00:12:08,990
What companies are finding that it's often necessary for you to upgrade your nodes.

119
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:19,190
Workloads are not typically like you still have complicated workloads that are not I would say cloud-native friendly, necessarily.

120
00:12:19,190 --> 00:12:22,950
And because of that, the upgrade process can have a couple bumps here and there.

121
00:12:22,950 --> 00:12:26,950
There's many companies that still have outages when they do upgrades.

122
00:12:26,950 --> 00:12:32,270
And you can't have a cloud provider automatically upgrade for you, necessarily.

123
00:12:32,270 --> 00:12:36,829
Nice theoretical world, we'd be able to have that.

124
00:12:36,829 --> 00:12:43,910
There's other packages within cloud providers where the cloud providers are maintaining your nodes for you. And then of course, upgrades do happen in the background.

125
00:12:43,910 --> 00:12:56,670
You're able to set up here's the window I want my upgrades to happen between 2:00 and 4:00 a.m. Eastern time on Saturdays, for instance, or on Tuesdays, cuz that's the lowest traffic day of the year or of a week that you have.

126
00:12:56,670 --> 00:12:58,430
So, again, there's options.

127
00:12:58,430 --> 00:13:03,470
Going back to what you're talking about originally, Robert, those are decisions you need to make before you set up your cluster.

128
00:13:03,470 --> 00:13:05,110
Do you want to maintain your own control plane?

129
00:13:05,110 --> 00:13:07,070
Please don't.

130
00:13:07,070 --> 00:13:08,910
Please don't, unless you really know what you're doing.

131
00:13:08,910 --> 00:13:10,990
Do you want to maintain your own node pools?

132
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:18,430
50/50 on that, depends the amount of staff you have, or do you want your cloud provider to completely maintain everything for you?

133
00:13:18,430 --> 00:13:21,710
And on top of that, then you're looking at less work, but you lose control.

134
00:13:21,710 --> 00:13:23,711
There's trade-offs there. Definitely trade-offs.

135
00:13:23,711 --> 00:13:29,990
Give some more details of what can go wrong during an upgrade.

136
00:13:29,990 --> 00:13:37,750
Theoretically, Kubernetes will reschedule traffic off of the nodes you want to remove and onto the new ones, but when does that not work as intended?

137
00:13:37,760 --> 00:13:42,350
Long-running jobs, for instance, that don't restart.

138
00:13:42,350 --> 00:13:51,790
So, stateful applications that aren't truly cloud-native, you can run into problems with more complicated stateful applications during an upgrade process.

139
00:13:51,790 --> 00:14:00,670
You need to do something to the database, for instance, put it into a certain type of mode before you upgrade before you evict the pod.

140
00:14:00,670 --> 00:14:06,230
So, sometimes you've got to do three steps before you upgrade, and then three steps after you upgrade.

141
00:14:06,230 --> 00:14:08,910
So, that makes it a little bit more complicated.

142
00:14:08,910 --> 00:14:13,910
Older database systems that have been made more cloud-friendly still have some challenges.

143
00:14:13,910 --> 00:14:26,030
So, stateful applications, long-running applications that don't necessarily restart themselves, or you have a 60-minute job, and you go through an upgrade, shouldn't be upgrading at that time cuz those jobs are running.

144
00:14:26,030 --> 00:14:28,150
Now you've got to rerun a 60-minute job.

145
00:14:28,150 --> 00:14:30,430
And if it's a critical process, it's kind of challenging.

146
00:14:30,430 --> 00:14:38,949
And you can run not only in upgrades, but you can run the same thing when you're running or run into the same type of problem when you're running auto-scaling.

147
00:14:38,949 --> 00:14:48,990
So, if you have a long-running job, such as a CI build, your auto-scaler sizes down the node pool, which in effect is the same type of behavior you do when you upgrade a Kubernetes cluster.

148
00:14:48,990 --> 00:14:50,790
You'll have that job kicked off.

149
00:14:50,790 --> 00:14:53,150
Hopefully, you want that job to restart, but sometimes they don't.

150
00:14:53,160 --> 00:15:01,910
From security perspective, the upgrade process is important that it goes quickly so that you can remediate security issues quickly.

151
00:15:01,910 --> 00:15:05,910
If you're looking at redeploying workloads, you're looking at pushing out new workloads.

152
00:15:05,920 --> 00:15:14,670
It's a combination of the processes that you have in-house to run upgrades, how complicated your workload is, as well as if you need to roll out new workload.

153
00:15:14,680 --> 00:15:26,670
Say you've got a spring dependency issue where there's a CV in spring and you need to roll out an entirely new application because of that or 40 applications.

154
00:15:26,670 --> 00:15:29,230
Again, your CI needs to be able to handle that type of roll out.

155
00:15:29,240 --> 00:15:33,790
I've been involved in database migrations some years ago.

156
00:15:33,790 --> 00:15:42,750
This typically involves a lot of runbooks, manual steps, like you described where the three things you do before and the three things you do after.

157
00:15:42,750 --> 00:15:55,069
I am aware that there are some offerings in the database world where they've created Kubernetes operators that to some extent can take over the role of a human system admin.

158
00:15:55,069 --> 00:16:01,829
Are most things in Kubernetes world now aiming to be pretty automated in face of all kinds of disruptions,

159
00:16:01,829 --> 00:16:09,430
or is it still kind of old-school where database is going to be migrated and we're going to have downtime and we're going to baby sit it?

160
00:16:09,430 --> 00:16:12,030
Well, it depends on what you're running, right?

161
00:16:12,030 --> 00:16:18,670
It's application and operator dependent, not necessarily Kubernetes cluster dependent, is the way I would put it.

162
00:16:18,670 --> 00:16:27,750
If the workload can restart itself nicely, you flip a button, ham it fault-tolerance mode, and you know that your fault tolerance works as expected, you should be fine.

163
00:16:27,750 --> 00:16:35,110
It's running the upgrade in dev first to make sure that you're fine, rather than having a 40-step runbook and playbook.

164
00:16:35,120 --> 00:16:37,750
Are we there yet?

165
00:16:37,750 --> 00:16:42,190
No. If you can afford it, have somebody else maintain your databases for you.

166
00:16:42,190 --> 00:16:47,510
Have people like I would say most companies aren't Google, aren't Apple, aren't Facebook, right?

167
00:16:47,520 --> 00:16:53,230
They don't have hundreds of engineers that maintain their databases for them necessarily.

168
00:16:53,230 --> 00:16:56,430
If you are that company, then yeah, you might be able to get away with it.

169
00:16:56,430 --> 00:17:04,669
The thing with running databases and other stateful applications is you have to understand how that stateful application actually runs on top of Kubernetes really well.

170
00:17:04,679 --> 00:17:09,710
It's not just I'm running an application and it runs great.

171
00:17:09,710 --> 00:17:14,710
It's I'm running an application on top of Kubernetes because of the way it fails over.

172
00:17:14,710 --> 00:17:25,590
And it basically if it says, "Okay, you got 60 seconds to leave, application A, B, C, or replica A, B, C is a better way to put it, you have 60 seconds and you're out of here.

173
00:17:25,590 --> 00:17:31,350
I don't care what's going on, this node's going down, and your pit is going to get terminated." So, your application has to handle that.

174
00:17:31,350 --> 00:17:39,590
And realistically, though, Robert, your application needs to handle that anyways because it's the same pattern that happens during outages.

175
00:17:39,590 --> 00:17:42,510
Again, it goes back.

176
00:17:42,510 --> 00:17:44,510
If your company can afford RDS, why not use RDS?

177
00:17:44,510 --> 00:17:50,870
If your company can afford running SQL Server on Azure, let the experts handle it.

178
00:17:50,870 --> 00:17:55,710
It turns over some control, you have to run their version of Postgres.

179
00:17:55,710 --> 00:17:58,430
Rather than, you know, you might have three versions of Postgres that you can run.

180
00:17:58,430 --> 00:18:02,110
So, if you need a much older version, then you might be running it yourself.

181
00:18:02,110 --> 00:18:07,030
But that's a whole 'nother story where let's have Postgres updated in your company.

182
00:18:07,030 --> 00:18:10,070
You know, it goes back to time and money, right?

183
00:18:10,070 --> 00:18:12,910
The story I tell about security is you're going to get hacked most likely.

184
00:18:12,910 --> 00:18:17,110
You're big, you're going to get hacked, and or people are going to be knocking on your door a lot.

185
00:18:17,110 --> 00:18:20,630
But it goes back to business risk and time and money.

186
00:18:20,630 --> 00:18:29,230
As much as we talk about these security offerings and what to do, it all goes back to how many hours a day we have to engineer it, how many hands we have on keyboards.

187
00:18:29,230 --> 00:18:31,590
And I would say now, we've definitely have some help from AI.

188
00:18:31,600 --> 00:18:36,310
I don't think it's quite there yet, but supposedly we're going to be out of a job here pretty soon, Robert.

189
00:18:36,310 --> 00:18:41,030
So, I don't believe that, but that's a whole 'nother topic that we don't need to go into.

190
00:18:41,030 --> 00:18:43,070
But like I said, it's all time and money.

191
00:18:43,070 --> 00:18:45,710
We can do this, it's not rocket science.

